00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.910
So let's walk
through what happens

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:28.180
when you try this app out.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:29.990
Here's a good example.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:34.740
It says, "The wolf and the wild
boar were first on the ground;

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.350
and when they came,
he gave them some

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:37.930
of the most annoying
problems known

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:41.660
to humanity: version-controlling
Microsoft Word documents."

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:44.100
And that's pretty--
that's pretty rough.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:44.620
ERIK MCCLENNEY: I
don't know if that's

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:45.890
the most annoying problem.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:46.914
Maybe one of them.

00:01:46.914 --> 00:01:49.080
SHARAT SHROFF: So you want
to just walk them through

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:49.945
on how it works?

00:01:49.945 --> 00:01:51.220
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Sure.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:54.270
What we're doing is we're
taking fragments from each

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.754
of the source sentences.

00:01:55.754 --> 00:01:57.920
And you can see, we kind
of have a little fancy font

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.490
for the "Grimms' Fairy Tales."

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.290
And we have kind
of a nice monospace

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:05.650
font for the "Git Handbook."

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:07.350
And for each of the
source fragments,

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.590
we've highlighted them
in the source sentence,

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:13.057
and then we've kind of chained
them together, as you see here.

00:02:13.057 --> 00:02:13.890
SHARAT SHROFF: Yeah.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:18.200
And sometimes, you can see,
it's taken about eight sentences

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.660
to make this.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.620
We've taken words
randomly across eight

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.880
different sentences,
assembled all of this.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.640
This is all happening
in a state machine

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.220
that's running on App Engine.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:33.640
And I've seen this app
sometimes take 12, 13 sentences,

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.530
sometimesas few as
three sentences.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.360
You can start sometimes
in "Git Handbook"

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.700
and go to "Grimms'," and
sometimes you can start from

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:43.640
"Grimms'" to "Git" and "Grimms'"
and so on, any combination.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.010
You know, let's skip
this another example

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.330
and tell you how the
app itself is built.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:51.724
It's a very straightforward,
very simple app.

00:02:51.724 --> 00:02:55.230
Your two bodies of text
that I was talking about

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.770
exist in GCS in two
different stores.

00:02:57.770 --> 00:02:59.910
We have a run time.

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:03.090
It's basically a Java app
