00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:09.270
Logs are very--
they're very flexible.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:12.330
We can record any
information that we want.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.426
In the case of App
Engine, they tell us

00:04:14.426 --> 00:04:15.550
how long our requests take.

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:17.899
We could start there.

00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:19.595
SHARAT SHROFF: Use Trace, maybe?

00:04:19.595 --> 00:04:21.470
ERIK MCCLENNEY: We also
have a Trace product.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:22.790
And it's convenient
because I happen

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:24.230
to have worked on this product.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.540
SHARAT SHROFF: Of course.

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:28.720
ERIK MCCLENNEY: It's designed
to show us [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.200
information for our
apps and kind of give us

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.407
a view into the service
RPCs that our apps call.

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:36.490
SHARAT SHROFF: So while
you're pulling up the app,

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:39.220
why don't I just-- could you
just go back to the slides?

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:49.000
I could just talk about-- so
when we think about performance

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:52.350
troubleshooting and
production, we've

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.270
heard engineers tell
that, of course,

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.430
you need to be able to
very accurately and very

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:02.110
precisely measure
requests, measure latency

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:04.070
at an individual request level.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:08.010
You also need to be able to
correlate logs so that you

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.860
actually get this
entire view of what's

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.410
happening within
your application

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:14.650
from a latency point of view.

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:18.150
And you need to be able to
look at different distribution

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:18.790
slices.

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:20.670
And what I mean by
that is, average

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:22.490
itself doesn't tell you much.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:24.760
You need to be able to look
at the 75th percentile.

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:28.780
When you look at
an average view,

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:31.610
an average aggregate view,
of the latency associated

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:34.440
with your application, you
may not notice any issues.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.630
But if you slice it down
to the 75th percentile

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:40.910
or the 95th percentile, you'll
start seeing those differences

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:43.630
in latencies, and that
becomes a place where

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:45.384
you want to start diving into.

00:05:45.384 --> 00:05:47.800
And the other thing that we
have heard customers often say

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:52.750
is, performance is something
that people end up-- engineers

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:56.580
end up reacting to while they
would much rather prefer being

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:01.160
proactive about this, to
be aware of the problems

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:04.590
even before it actually
impacts the customers.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.700
So with all that in
mind, we ended up

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:10.110
building Stackdriver Trace.

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:16.680
And for Stackdriver Trace, we
started off with, of course,

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:22.540
providing this very-- we
automatically trace every end

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.450
point that you have.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:30.100
And we are able to surface that
within the Stackdriver Trace

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.010
tool.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.990
We also overlaid this log.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:35.990
So we can basically--
in this diagram what

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:38.510
you see here is,
basically, we tell you

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:40.770
what's happening across
here the entire time taken

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:43.820
for your entire request.

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:47.930
And then we also break it down
into its constituent parts

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:51.700
and tell you where exactly
the time is being spent.

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:58.141
We are able to also break
down by different distribution

00:06:58.141 --> 00:06:58.640
slices.

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:01.790
So you can actually
tell, for example,

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:04.930
what is your application
latency of the 25th percentile,

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:06.972
at the 75th or the
50th percentile.

00:07:06.972 --> 00:07:09.180
And we're also able to tell
you whether it's actually

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.550
going up or down.

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:13.760
Erik will show you some
of these in a second.

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:19.060
And we have spent
a bunch of time

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:24.770
looking at being able to provide
analysis on all of these.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:28.817
And Erik, I think we should just
actually show them the tool.

00:07:28.817 --> 00:07:29.900
ERIK MCCLENNEY: All right.

00:07:34.141 --> 00:07:35.640
Maybe you could
drive here while I--

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:37.765
SHARAT SHROFF: Yeah,
actually, why don't I do that?

00:07:37.765 --> 00:07:41.000
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Here we have
our Trace Overview page.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.820
And the page is
organized in a number

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:45.070
of cards that are
designed to give

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:48.740
different views into the trace
data that we have for the app.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:52.490
On the upper right side,
we have our Insights card.

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:55.681
And this shows some
of the insights

00:07:55.681 --> 00:07:56.930
that Sharat just talked about.

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:58.513
And we'll look at
this in more detail.

00:07:58.513 --> 00:08:02.490
We see one that's called
many datastore.next calls.

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:05.100
Maybe that will come up
later in our conversation.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:07.180
And then below that,
we have a few lists

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:09.740
of traces that are
organized on different ways.

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:15.032
The first one, the traces
are organized by recency.

00:08:15.032 --> 00:08:16.490
And then below
that, we have traces

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:20.490
organized both by
frequency of request URL

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:24.870
and by frequency of
App Engine service RPC.

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:28.054
On the right side, we have
our list of analysis reports.

00:08:28.054 --> 00:08:29.970
This is what we were
showing just a moment ago

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:31.260
in the slides.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.350
The analysis reports are
capable of running over

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:35.169
large numbers of traces.

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:39.460
And they can show you
a latency histogram

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:42.340
for your app for the
requests that it handles.

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:44.650
And what we do on
the daily reports.

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:46.986
These ones are
generated automatically.

00:08:46.986 --> 00:08:54.280
And if we have enough data,
we can show two reports.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:55.900
In this case, for
the daily reports

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:59.550
we have the first day,
which is yesterday.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.430
And then we have a
week before yesterday.

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:04.610
And we're kind of showing you
those two reports side by side.

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:07.220
So this can show you if you
have a latency shift, perhaps,

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:07.936
in your app.

00:09:07.936 --> 00:09:09.560
SHARAT SHROFF: It's
probably also worth

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:13.680
noting that this report, this
comparison between reports,

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:18.000
need not be just
done on a time basis.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.040
You can do it on a
version-to-version basis.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.450
So we can generate
reports saying,

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:26.310
tell me my application
profile and compare it

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:28.852
with what it was with
my previous version.

00:09:28.852 --> 00:09:30.060
ERIK MCCLENNEY: That's right.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:32.476
And for the daily reports, the
ones that are automatically

00:09:32.476 --> 00:09:35.690
generated, we
choose from requests

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:37.820
that were sent to the
