00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:41.200
That's another place that
we use that information.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:42.060
All right.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:45.970
Well, when we try to
generate our sentence,

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:48.280
that took a little longer
than we thought it should.

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:53.780
So now we're going to look at a
trace detail for the sentence.

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:56.800
So we can see here, we've got
some long-running requests here

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:58.080
for sentence.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:09:59.930
That one took almost 19 seconds.

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:02.180
We're going to look at the
Trace Detail page for this.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:03.470
SHARAT SHROFF: Should
I click on that?

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:04.400
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Yes, you should.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:05.150
SHARAT SHROFF: OK.

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:11.370
Should we start
using Trace on our--

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:12.550
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Maybe we should
use Trace to diagnose this?

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:13.990
SHARAT SHROFF: Should use our
own feature on our service?

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:15.460
Is that what you're saying?

00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:16.220
ERIK MCCLENNEY: I'm
not saying that.

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:17.011
You're saying that.

00:10:17.011 --> 00:10:18.340
But it's a good idea.

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:20.620
But actually, there's
something going on here,

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.001
and we'll talk about that, too.

00:10:23.872 --> 00:10:25.330
SHARAT SHROFF: Or
is it just trying

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:26.470
to retrieve a whole
bunch of data?

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:28.719
ERIK MCCLENNEY: It's trying
to retrieve a lot of data.

00:10:28.719 --> 00:10:30.140
So here we see our trace.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:35.060
Now a trace is a bucket--
basically, a group of spins.

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:37.770
And the spins have
some basic information.

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:38.950
They have a name.

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:41.767
And then we get the latency
information by the timing

00:10:41.767 --> 00:10:42.850
that we have in the spins.

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:45.470
There's both a start
time and an end time.

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:48.030
So we can organize
spins kind of in space.

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:50.870
And we also have the
latency for them.

00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:52.700
And they also have a hierarchy.

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:55.770
So the request here, the
entire request duration,

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:58.520
we see in the first spin,
called slash sentence.

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:00.020
And then all the
ones below that are

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:02.020
children of that parent spin.

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:04.480
So it gives us a
hierarchical representation

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:05.679
of what our app is doing.

00:11:05.679 --> 00:11:07.595
SHARAT SHROFF: There's
a huge number of these.

00:11:07.595 --> 00:11:09.636
ERIK MCCLENNEY: There is
a large number of those.

00:11:09.636 --> 00:11:12.440
And I think probably the page
load was because we're actually

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:14.530
loading probably
thousands of these things

00:11:14.530 --> 00:11:16.942
and showing them
here in our browser.

00:11:16.942 --> 00:11:18.900
So in fact, there's so
many, I mean, you barely

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:20.360
see that little
scroll thumb moving.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:21.220
I can't even count these.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:23.320
So why don't we click on
the Summary tab there?

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:24.845
SHARAT SHROFF: OK.

00:11:24.845 --> 00:11:26.470
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Now
on the Summary tab,

00:11:26.470 --> 00:11:30.000
it shows us by request
and service RPC

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.010
just how many of these
spins that we see.

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:36.140
In this case, we see over
2,000 datastore.next calls.

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:40.482
Below that we see that we
have eight run query calls.

00:11:40.482 --> 00:11:41.940
And I think, with
this information,

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:44.357
I have kind of an idea
of what our app is doing.

00:11:44.357 --> 00:11:46.190
And let me just go back
to the Grimm Git app

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:46.960
here for just a second.

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:48.001
We don't need to show it.

00:11:48.001 --> 00:11:49.970
I'm just going to
explain what it's doing.

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:54.024
What it does to generate that
list of source sentences is,

00:11:54.024 --> 00:11:56.690
you've got a large state machine
with all the different sentence

00:11:56.690 --> 00:11:57.356
fragments in it.

00:11:57.356 --> 00:11:59.860
And we just kind of
walk that to generate

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:01.760
the generated sentence.

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:05.980
But to come up with the source
sentences for each source that

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:08.430
was chosen for the node
for that sentence fragment,

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:10.890
we hash the sentence.

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:12.735
And we store that
hash in the node.

00:12:12.735 --> 00:12:14.610
We do this because we
have a lot of sentences

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:15.401
and a lot of nodes.

00:12:15.401 --> 00:12:19.550
And we don't want to replicate
all the sentences everywhere.

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:21.720
And then we look
into our data store

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:24.260
by hash to find each of
the source sentences.

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:26.110
So in this case, we
had a sentence that

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:27.400
had eight source sentences.

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:29.380
And we did eight queries.

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:32.890
But for some reason, that
turned into over 2,000

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:33.939
datastore.next calls.

00:12:33.939 --> 00:12:34.730
SHARAT SHROFF: Wow.

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:36.290
ERIK MCCLENNEY: And this is
