00:16:12.837 --> 00:16:14.420
But what this is
doing is it's letting

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:19.460
me take a look at the code that
I have for my find sentence

00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:20.550
method.

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:23.450
This is the method that
goes through the datastore.

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:27.110
As I mentioned, it kind
of looks for sentences

00:16:27.110 --> 00:16:28.649
with a matching hash.

00:16:28.649 --> 00:16:29.940
And let's look at what it does.

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:33.500
It first sets up a
query, and then it

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:35.950
goes through all
the entities it gets

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:38.790
from the iterator from
that iterable there.

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:42.200
And for each entity, it
looks at the value property.

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:45.810
The value property is the
source sentence itself.

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:47.890
And then we hash the sentence.

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:49.890
And then we compare the
hashes for equality.

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:51.680
The hash that we
pass into the method

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:54.000
is the one that we got
out of our state machine.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:58.680
And it's curious to me because
I remember working on this.

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:02.280
And I know that when I write
the entities into the datastore,

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:04.500
I actually write a
hash property there.

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:06.569
So right away you can
see, we're computing

00:17:06.569 --> 00:17:09.359
a hash for the sentence
from the datastore.

00:17:09.359 --> 00:17:11.270
But that's not the only thing.

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:13.010
By adding the hash
property, by default

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:14.960
the datastore gives
us a secondary index

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:16.339
on that property.

00:17:16.339 --> 00:17:19.349
So I can actually apply a filter
to my query and do a lookup.

00:17:19.349 --> 00:17:22.599
What this is doing by iterating
through the table this way,

00:17:22.599 --> 00:17:25.960
we're actually potentially
iterating over all the entities

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:28.810
in the table for this kind.

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:31.830
And we know the hash already
for what we're looking for.

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:34.090
So we should be able
to just do a lookup.

00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:36.800
Now as I mentioned, by default
you don't see anything here

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:38.950
about batch size.

00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:41.010
The default batch size is 20.

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:46.640
And what the SDK code is doing
is it's saying, all right,

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:49.230
we're going to query this
table for these entities.

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:53.760
And it's fetching ahead the
batch size number of entities.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:56.260
And it's holding those there
as we iterate through them.

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:59.634
And when we exhaust that, it
goes and gets the next batch.

00:17:59.634 --> 00:18:01.550
So you've got to have
some default batch size.

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:04.280
And 20 is probably good
for some common use cases,

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:06.337
like maybe a web page
or the tabular view.

00:18:06.337 --> 00:18:07.170
SHARAT SHROFF: Yeah.

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:08.086
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Right?

00:18:08.086 --> 00:18:10.010
In this case, we know
that we're iterating

00:18:10.010 --> 00:18:11.540
through the entire table.

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:13.540
So 20 is probably
too small, given

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:17.260
that we have thousands of
sentences in this table.

00:18:17.260 --> 00:18:18.980
So what I'm going
to do is replace

00:18:18.980 --> 00:18:21.920
where we call this with a
call to another method that

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:23.320
applies a filter.

00:18:23.320 --> 00:18:24.880
And having planned
ahead, I actually

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:26.170
have such a method down below.

00:18:26.170 --> 00:18:29.650
Maybe you could scroll down
a little bit past all these

00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:31.910
to-- keep going, keep going.

00:18:31.910 --> 00:18:32.521
Don't be shy.

00:18:32.521 --> 00:18:33.020
Keep going.

00:18:33.020 --> 00:18:33.978
All right, right there.

00:18:33.978 --> 00:18:36.090
Find sentence with filter.

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:40.520
This one adds a
filter to our query

00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:43.180
and adds a predicate with
an equality operator.

00:18:43.180 --> 00:18:46.480
So we're looking for
entities with the property

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:49.346
hash that equals the
hash that we pass in.

00:18:49.346 --> 00:18:51.720
And they should translate into
a datastore lookup instead

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:52.674
of a scan.

00:18:52.674 --> 00:18:54.590
You won't be scanning
through the whole table.

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:56.395
Chunks of 20 sentences
at a time here.

00:18:56.395 --> 00:18:59.320
We'll go straight
to our sentence.

