00:24:18.272 --> 00:24:20.200
ERIK MCCLENNEY:
Probably a lot of logs.

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:22.229
SHARAT SHROFF: Stackdriver
Error Reporting?

00:24:22.229 --> 00:24:24.020
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Yes,
that's why we're here.

00:24:24.020 --> 00:24:26.586
We'll talk about
error reporting.

00:24:26.586 --> 00:24:27.460
SHARAT SHROFF: Right.

00:24:27.460 --> 00:24:33.110
So let's talk about
error reporting.

00:24:33.110 --> 00:24:38.160
So as Erik was just describing,
I mean, we could certainly

00:24:38.160 --> 00:24:39.350
go look at logs.

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:41.840
You've got errors scattered
everywhere, all over logs.

00:24:41.840 --> 00:24:44.920
And it's really difficult
to get this overall view.

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:48.770
It's also difficult to evaluate
the impact of just looking

00:24:48.770 --> 00:24:50.981
at errors within the logs.

00:24:50.981 --> 00:24:51.480
So great.

00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:54.260
If you do find an error
in a log, what do you do?

00:24:54.260 --> 00:24:57.680
Do you basically stop everything
and start trying to fix it?

00:24:57.680 --> 00:25:02.570
I mean, what is the best way to
gauge when you encounter errors

00:25:02.570 --> 00:25:03.130
in the log?

00:25:03.130 --> 00:25:05.460
It's always been
a difficult thing.

00:25:05.460 --> 00:25:07.950
Discovering new issues
takes a long time.

00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:09.950
Very often you get to
know about issues only

00:25:09.950 --> 00:25:12.720
after your customers
have already seen it

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:14.390
and start complaining.

00:25:14.390 --> 00:25:16.860
And it's difficult
to validate often

00:25:16.860 --> 00:25:20.130
whether the fixes that you
have made in the version

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:23.423
that you're about to ship out
have actually solved the error

00:25:23.423 --> 00:25:24.410
or not.

00:25:24.410 --> 00:25:28.500
And I mean, if
anyone is-- I'm sure

00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:31.970
everyone here has probably
looked at error log statement.

00:25:31.970 --> 00:25:34.340
And there's just so
much noise in there.

00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:37.660
There is so much stuff in
there, irrelevant, very often

00:25:37.660 --> 00:25:42.700
irrelevant stuff, that, I guess,
my eyes tend to glaze over.

00:25:42.700 --> 00:25:46.950
So what we did is,
this morning we

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:51.650
launched publicly
Stackdriver Error Reporting.

00:25:51.650 --> 00:25:55.925
If there's one takeaway I
want everyone to have here is,

00:25:55.925 --> 00:25:58.300
if there's a page that you
want to pin to your favorites,

00:25:58.300 --> 00:26:01.107
it's probably Error Reporting.

00:26:01.107 --> 00:26:03.440
I'll just walk through the
various parts really quickly,

00:26:03.440 --> 00:26:05.900
and then we can just start
looking at the tool itself.

00:26:05.900 --> 00:26:08.330
There's a lot of
information, and our goal

00:26:08.330 --> 00:26:14.070
is to give you at-a-glance view
of all the errors happening

00:26:14.070 --> 00:26:16.750
within your application.

00:26:16.750 --> 00:26:19.530
We go about taking
all the errors

00:26:19.530 --> 00:26:23.120
that encounter across all the
instances across all the logs--

00:26:23.120 --> 00:26:24.820
logs across all instances.

00:26:24.820 --> 00:26:26.910
And we group them
intelligently so

00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:30.210
that you don't have to worry
about how often it's occurring.

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:35.690
We allow you to slice it
by different time periods.

00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:38.630
You can slice it by
different versions.

00:26:38.630 --> 00:26:41.950
We have an easy way to show
that a new error has occurred,

00:26:41.950 --> 00:26:44.810
a very intuitive way
to immediately spot

00:26:44.810 --> 00:26:46.870
when a new error has occurred.

00:26:46.870 --> 00:26:48.738
We try to tell you,
when available,

00:26:48.738 --> 00:26:51.196
the customer impact, how many
customers are being impacted.

00:26:53.720 --> 00:26:55.840
You can sign up
for notifications.

00:26:55.840 --> 00:27:00.560
And you can link it to your
own backtracking system.

00:27:00.560 --> 00:27:03.460
So all of this goes-- there
is a tremendous amount

00:27:03.460 --> 00:27:04.530
of information here.

00:27:04.530 --> 00:27:08.940
But I think, why don't we just
actually see it in action?

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:10.299
Oh, actually, before we do that.

00:27:10.299 --> 00:27:10.840
Let me just--

00:27:10.840 --> 00:27:13.610
ERIK MCCLENNEY: See,
there's an action first.

00:27:13.610 --> 00:27:15.360
SHARAT SHROFF: So
this is, again,

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:18.140
a very high-level view of
how Error Reporting works.

00:27:18.140 --> 00:27:20.700
In just a ton of logs,
there's a big black box,

00:27:20.700 --> 00:27:23.600
or blue box in this case, which
does a bunch of processing

00:27:23.600 --> 00:27:24.420
and grouping.

00:27:24.420 --> 00:27:27.530
And we basically--
output of that

00:27:27.530 --> 00:27:29.640
is this intelligent
grouping that we'll

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:32.100
see in action in a few minutes.

00:27:32.100 --> 00:27:34.100
That, of course, goes to
a service, which allows

00:27:34.100 --> 00:27:35.224
us to do a bunch of things.

00:27:35.224 --> 00:27:43.240
And as well as surface this
to our UI using an API.

00:27:43.240 --> 00:27:46.150
And that API, just like on the
trace, is available for you

00:27:46.150 --> 00:27:48.275
to ingest into your
own third-party tools.

00:27:48.275 --> 00:27:49.650
And of course, we
have a notifier

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:53.462
that sends you notification
when there's a new error.

00:27:53.462 --> 00:27:54.775
Should we look at the tool?

00:27:54.775 --> 00:27:56.275
ERIK MCCLENNEY:
Yeah, let's do that.

00:28:01.957 --> 00:28:03.290
And one thing I want to mention.

00:28:03.290 --> 00:28:08.370
All these can be found here
on the left, down here, where

00:28:08.370 --> 00:28:09.950
it says Stackdriver.

00:28:09.950 --> 00:28:12.830
You've got Debug, which
we showed you briefly

00:28:12.830 --> 00:28:13.420
a second ago.

00:28:13.420 --> 00:28:15.211
You've got Trace and
Error Reporting, which

00:28:15.211 --> 00:28:16.345
we're talking about today.

00:28:16.345 --> 00:28:17.720
Logging, which
we're not bashing.

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:20.095
Logging is great, but I'm
going to keep bringing this up.

00:28:20.095 --> 00:28:23.980
And we've got Monitoring under
the Stackdriver section there.

00:28:23.980 --> 00:28:25.400
And you can also
pin these things.

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:25.970
SHARAT SHROFF: Exactly.

00:28:25.970 --> 00:28:26.820
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Is that
what you were going to say?

00:28:26.820 --> 00:28:28.400
SHARAT SHROFF: That's exactly
what I was about to say.

00:28:28.400 --> 00:28:29.816
ERIK MCCLENNEY:
We practiced this.

00:28:29.816 --> 00:28:34.670
This is why we're-- but on the
