00:29:27.230 --> 00:29:29.260
We have a blown-up histogram.

00:29:29.260 --> 00:29:32.010
As you go through this,
you'll see the frequency

00:29:32.010 --> 00:29:35.940
that we've had over time
for this error type.

00:29:35.940 --> 00:29:41.540
And then for each type of error,
we have a number of samples.

00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:46.020
And for each sample, we have
some additional information.

00:29:46.020 --> 00:29:48.960
And this kind of is
information about the request

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:51.650
or the environment that
the app is running in.

00:29:51.650 --> 00:29:54.220
And this is designed to help
us reproduce this problem.

00:29:54.220 --> 00:29:56.660
We can see we've got
the full request URL.

00:29:56.660 --> 00:30:01.134
We've got the status,
which we saw firsthand.

00:30:01.134 --> 00:30:02.300
Then we've got some headers.

00:30:02.300 --> 00:30:04.200
We've got the user
agent with refer.

00:30:04.200 --> 00:30:06.390
And App Engine
information, this default

00:30:06.390 --> 00:30:08.240
is the App Engine module.

00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:10.790
And then we have the version
that the app was running

00:30:10.790 --> 00:30:13.680
when it handled this request.

00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:16.419
Now below this, we
have a stack trace.

00:30:16.419 --> 00:30:18.460
But this stack trace looks
different from the one

00:30:18.460 --> 00:30:21.580
that we saw in cloud trace,
in Stackdriver Trace.

00:30:21.580 --> 00:30:22.200
Why is that?

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:23.130
SHARAT SHROFF: It's cleaner.

00:30:23.130 --> 00:30:23.750
ERIK MCCLENNEY: It is cleaner.

00:30:23.750 --> 00:30:24.380
SHARAT SHROFF: It's much better.

00:30:24.380 --> 00:30:26.380
ERIK MCCLENNEY: It's a
lot different to my eyes.

00:30:26.380 --> 00:30:29.070
Well, if I expand this, you'll
see what's happened here.

00:30:29.070 --> 00:30:32.680
As I said before, in our stack
trace we have many frames.

00:30:32.680 --> 00:30:34.850
A lot of them are kind
of infrastructure.

00:30:34.850 --> 00:30:36.590
It doesn't mean that
the bug we're seeing

00:30:36.590 --> 00:30:38.697
doesn't happen there,
but it's probably

00:30:38.697 --> 00:30:41.030
more likely that it's happening
in our application code,

00:30:41.030 --> 00:30:43.140
for us to be honest
with ourselves.

00:30:43.140 --> 00:30:45.500
So here we've kind
of formatted this.

00:30:45.500 --> 00:30:49.450
And we've grayed out the kind
of infrastructure frames.

00:30:49.450 --> 00:30:51.510
And then we just show
it with the ability

00:30:51.510 --> 00:30:55.975
to collapse it so that we just
see our frames from our app.

00:30:55.975 --> 00:30:57.350
So this is very
useful in helping

00:30:57.350 --> 00:31:00.344
us kind of just visually see
where our errors are happening.

00:31:00.344 --> 00:31:01.760
SHARAT SHROFF:
That's exactly what

00:31:01.760 --> 00:31:03.840
I was talking about
where, if you just

00:31:03.840 --> 00:31:06.130
show the raw information
there, I mean, it's

00:31:06.130 --> 00:31:07.370
difficult to discern.

00:31:07.370 --> 00:31:10.830
And I think that's really
cool that you can actually

00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:12.905
zero in on exactly
where the problem is.

00:31:12.905 --> 00:31:13.780
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Yeah.

00:31:13.780 --> 00:31:16.130
And now looking
at this, I think I

00:31:16.130 --> 00:31:18.515
have an idea of where
this bug is happening.

00:31:18.515 --> 00:31:19.514
SHARAT SHROFF: Oh, yeah?

00:31:19.514 --> 00:31:21.477
Where is it?

00:31:21.477 --> 00:31:23.810
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Apparently,
it's on [INAUDIBLE] servlet,

00:31:23.810 --> 00:31:27.350
that class, a method
called doGet line 122.

00:31:27.350 --> 00:31:30.950
SHARAT SHROFF: I mean, how
are you going to confirm that?

00:31:30.950 --> 00:31:33.829
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Well, one
option would be to use logs.

00:31:33.829 --> 00:31:35.370
I said I was going
to say this again.

00:31:35.370 --> 00:31:37.630
And of course, we
could go in there

00:31:37.630 --> 00:31:40.960
and we can instrument
our code some more.

00:31:40.960 --> 00:31:42.840
We can try to guess it.

00:31:42.840 --> 00:31:44.260
This is a null
pointer exception,

00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:47.119
so we can see if we're
dereferencing something that

00:31:47.119 --> 00:31:48.285
looks like it might be null.

00:31:48.285 --> 00:31:49.710
And we can print the logs out.

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:50.490
We can build.

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:51.300
We can redeploy.

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:53.001
We can hit the app again.

00:31:53.001 --> 00:31:53.750
It's a lot of fun.

00:31:53.750 --> 00:31:57.650
SHARAT SHROFF: So that
involves you writing logs, then

00:31:57.650 --> 00:32:01.890
building and deploying, and
then again getting the logs,

00:32:01.890 --> 00:32:03.280
reviewing them.

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:05.997
And so we're going to keep
doing this for every bug?

00:32:05.997 --> 00:32:07.830
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Just
this one to start with.

00:32:07.830 --> 00:32:09.913
But wouldn't it be great
if we had a tool, though,

00:32:09.913 --> 00:32:12.300
where we could see this
happening while it's running?

00:32:12.300 --> 00:32:13.841
SHARAT SHROFF: And
on cue we're going

00:32:13.841 --> 00:32:15.111
to talk about Cloud Debugger.

00:32:15.111 --> 00:32:16.152
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Debugger.

00:32:16.152 --> 00:32:17.210
Excellent.

00:32:17.210 --> 00:32:20.057
And I said this was
going to come up again.

00:32:20.057 --> 00:32:21.890
SHARAT SHROFF: We could
talk about Debugger.

00:32:21.890 --> 00:32:23.410
I mean, I could go
through more slides.

00:32:23.410 --> 00:32:25.280
But why don't we just show
them the tool in action?

00:32:25.280 --> 00:32:25.570
ERIK MCCLENNEY: OK, that's good.

00:32:25.570 --> 00:32:26.970
SHARAT SHROFF: And I could
try to cover the slides later.

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:29.020
ERIK MCCLENNEY:
That's a good idea.

00:32:29.020 --> 00:32:30.770
Now here again we
have another link.

00:32:30.770 --> 00:32:32.990
And this will take us
to what we saw before.

00:32:32.990 --> 00:32:36.220
And this time we'll stay in
Debugger for a little longer.

00:32:36.220 --> 00:32:38.700
Now what's happening
here, we see

00:32:38.700 --> 00:32:40.350
our source that's loaded here.

00:32:40.350 --> 00:32:42.450
And just to let you
know, the Debugger

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:46.200
has different options for
getting access to source code.

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:48.507
In this case, we're using
our Cloud Source Repository.

00:32:48.507 --> 00:32:50.090
We've got the source
code loaded here.

00:32:50.090 --> 00:32:53.100
So it knows by kind
of module and version

00:32:53.100 --> 00:32:56.980
and, by the deployment,
what's running here.

00:32:56.980 --> 00:33:01.770
It can also read code
from local source.

00:33:01.770 --> 00:33:05.552
In this case, it doesn't
actually send any of the source

00:33:05.552 --> 00:33:06.260
to Google at all.

00:33:06.260 --> 00:33:08.746
It just loads the source
in the browser session

00:33:08.746 --> 00:33:10.120
and gets the
information it needs

00:33:10.120 --> 00:33:14.230
to set these snapshots here.

00:33:14.230 --> 00:33:17.490
In fact, I think we can
even do that without any--

00:33:17.490 --> 00:33:18.350
SHARAT SHROFF: Yeah.

00:33:18.350 --> 00:33:22.910
I mean, let's assume that, if
you don't have access to source

00:33:22.910 --> 00:33:26.150
code at all, you
can just give it

00:33:26.150 --> 00:33:29.155
a filename and a line
number, and it'll still

00:33:29.155 --> 00:33:31.632
take a snapshot.

00:33:31.632 --> 00:33:34.215
And when you take a snapshot,
as we did, what I was doing here

00:33:34.215 --> 00:33:39.110
is I was just reproing the
same error on my laptop here,

00:33:39.110 --> 00:33:40.170
and we got a snapshot.

00:33:40.170 --> 00:33:42.870
This is happening in production.

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:45.750
It is not impacting
any of the users.

00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:50.010
So we are basically-- we're
adding minimal overhead

00:33:50.010 --> 00:33:51.640
to your application.

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:54.060
Again, we're thinking about
single-digit milliseconds

00:33:54.060 --> 00:33:55.470
or microseconds.

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:58.870
It doesn't even matter
whether your application

00:33:58.870 --> 00:34:02.690
is running on one instance
or 100,000 instances.

00:34:02.690 --> 00:34:05.500
We will still be able
to capture a snapshot

00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:08.974
and capture the entire
application state,

00:34:08.974 --> 00:34:11.739
no matter which instance it
is, and then bring it back.

00:34:11.739 --> 00:34:13.530
ERIK MCCLENNEY: Yeah,
and that's important.

00:34:13.530 --> 00:34:15.154
When we think about
debuggers, we often

00:34:15.154 --> 00:34:18.230
think about setting a
breakpoint and then halting

00:34:18.230 --> 00:34:21.620
execution of the code and maybe
playing around with the machine

00:34:21.620 --> 00:34:24.489
state, setting registers,
backing up the code pointer,

00:34:24.489 --> 00:34:26.500
running it again.

00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:28.560
And that's great
in some situations

00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:30.699
when we have a dedicated
machine where we

00:34:30.699 --> 00:34:32.830
can reproduce these problems.

00:34:32.830 --> 00:34:37.719
Often with cloud apps, we don't
have that kind of flexibility.

00:34:37.719 --> 00:34:39.670
Some of the problems
that we see may

00:34:39.670 --> 00:34:43.960
be due to maybe resource
limitations or lots of user

00:34:43.960 --> 00:34:44.460
traffic.

00:34:44.460 --> 00:34:47.352
We may not easily be able to
reproduce these problems when

00:34:47.352 --> 00:34:49.060
they happen in
distributed apps this way.

00:34:49.060 --> 00:34:51.420
So what a snapshot
will let us do

00:34:51.420 --> 00:34:55.180
is capture a view of the machine
state on whatever machine

00:34:55.180 --> 00:34:57.240
that the snapshot's taken from.

00:34:57.240 --> 00:34:59.140
And it'll save it away
in a session for us

00:34:59.140 --> 00:35:01.160
to look at any time.

00:35:01.160 --> 00:35:03.120
And it won't interfere
with the operation,

00:35:03.120 --> 00:35:04.620
as we just mentioned.

00:35:04.620 --> 00:35:08.150
So as Sharat said, while
we were doing that,

00:35:08.150 --> 00:35:09.400
he actually hit the app again.

00:35:09.400 --> 00:35:11.420
I see a big red box there.

00:35:11.420 --> 00:35:12.885
And we took the snapshot.

00:35:12.885 --> 00:35:15.010
You may have noticed it
said, waiting for snapshot.

00:35:15.010 --> 00:35:17.734
And we executed this code.

00:35:17.734 --> 00:35:19.400
So now I'm going to
rearrange the screen

00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:23.270
a little bit so we can get a
little better view on this.

00:35:23.270 --> 00:35:25.475
Well, I guess that's
as low as that goes.

00:35:25.475 --> 00:35:26.800
This one.

00:35:26.800 --> 00:35:29.295
There you go.

00:35:29.295 --> 00:35:32.110
Should be able to do that.

00:35:32.110 --> 00:35:33.080
OK.

00:35:33.080 --> 00:35:36.200
Well, I'll just scroll
through these guys.

00:35:36.200 --> 00:35:39.510
As we can see, we've got
local variables here.

00:35:39.510 --> 00:35:41.820
You can see you've got--
this is an instance method.

00:35:41.820 --> 00:35:46.280
So we have a This pointer.

00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:51.630
And then we have our request
and response parameters there.

00:35:51.630 --> 00:35:53.527
They're passed in.

00:35:53.527 --> 00:35:55.360
Then we have some local
variables down here.

00:35:55.360 --> 00:35:59.110
We have vote LOL because
we pressed the LOL button

00:35:59.110 --> 00:36:00.840
to make this thing explode.

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:02.380
We have current null.

00:36:02.380 --> 00:36:05.622
It's probably what we're
interested in here.

00:36:05.622 --> 00:36:07.330
So we're going to take
a look at the code

00:36:07.330 --> 00:36:09.160
and see what we're doing.

00:36:09.160 --> 00:36:12.810
Now, thinking back on this,
I remember what I did.

00:36:12.810 --> 00:36:18.290
I think, as we improve upon
this sentence generator,

00:36:18.290 --> 00:36:21.330
we're going to keep track of
the quality of the sentences

00:36:21.330 --> 00:36:22.790
that it produces.

00:36:22.790 --> 00:36:25.784
So we have these voting
buttons, and eventually we're

00:36:25.784 --> 00:36:27.950
