00:38:36.170 --> 00:38:37.380
the same-- ah, there we go.

00:38:37.380 --> 00:38:39.120
This is what I was
trying to do before.

00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:41.704
And the same variables.

00:38:41.704 --> 00:38:42.870
SHARAT SHROFF: That's right.

00:38:42.870 --> 00:38:47.680
I mean, the ability for you to
share your debugging sessions

00:38:47.680 --> 00:38:49.210
or any of your
diagnosis sessions

00:38:49.210 --> 00:38:52.860
across any of these tools is
as easy as a copy and a paste.

00:38:52.860 --> 00:38:59.540
And if the person receiving it
has access to those resources,

00:38:59.540 --> 00:39:02.300
they'll just be able to
see the exact same data.

00:39:02.300 --> 00:39:04.894
And in this case, you can see
the entire-- all the variables.

00:39:04.894 --> 00:39:05.810
You can see the stack.

00:39:05.810 --> 00:39:08.090
You can see everything.

00:39:08.090 --> 00:39:10.690
So it makes it really
easy for engineers

00:39:10.690 --> 00:39:13.820
to collaborate,
which often happens,

00:39:13.820 --> 00:39:16.330
in order to
troubleshoot your app.

00:39:16.330 --> 00:39:17.710
ERIK MCCLENNEY: That's right.

00:39:17.710 --> 00:39:18.751
SHARAT SHROFF: All right.

00:39:18.751 --> 00:39:20.773
So it looks like you
can fix that bug later.

00:39:20.773 --> 00:39:22.485
Should we just go
back to the slides?

00:39:29.290 --> 00:39:31.709
So I actually went
through all of these.

00:39:31.709 --> 00:39:33.250
I think we went
through all of these.

00:39:33.250 --> 00:39:35.291
So I think the slides are
a little bit redundant.

00:39:38.190 --> 00:39:44.620
And I think I'm just going to
go to-- this is again redundant.

00:39:44.620 --> 00:39:46.380
I think the only thing
I'll call out here

00:39:46.380 --> 00:39:49.410
is, we have many
source code options.

00:39:49.410 --> 00:39:50.740
I think Erik talked about it.

00:39:50.740 --> 00:39:53.952
We've got local and cloud repo.

00:39:53.952 --> 00:39:55.160
And you can upload your code.

00:39:55.160 --> 00:39:57.410
And when you do
upload your code,

00:39:57.410 --> 00:40:00.940
that code is stored by GCP.

00:40:00.940 --> 00:40:03.360
And it's available for the
entire debugging session.

00:40:03.360 --> 00:40:04.890
So when you do a share,
you close your browser,

00:40:04.890 --> 00:40:06.500
come back later,
and somebody else--

00:40:06.500 --> 00:40:07.958
or you share it
with somebody else,

00:40:07.958 --> 00:40:09.582
that code is available for you.

00:40:09.582 --> 00:40:11.690
You can do with
no source at all.

00:40:11.690 --> 00:40:16.910
Just give a filename and a
line number and that's it.

00:40:16.910 --> 00:40:19.730
Again, a very-- it's
like a [INAUDIBLE] view

00:40:19.730 --> 00:40:21.000
of how Debugger works.

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:26.250
We've got a debugger service,
we've got debugger agents.

00:40:26.250 --> 00:40:28.320
And we've got an
agent controller.

00:40:28.320 --> 00:40:31.350
When you set up a snapshot,
your debugger service

00:40:31.350 --> 00:40:33.720
coordinates with the debugger
agent, takes a snapshot,

00:40:33.720 --> 00:40:40.988
sends it back, and you basically
see what we just demoed.

00:40:44.660 --> 00:40:46.080
So just to summarize.

00:40:46.080 --> 00:40:47.750
When you're diagnosing
an application,

00:40:47.750 --> 00:40:51.825
hopefully it's pretty
clear for everyone

00:40:51.825 --> 00:40:54.555
that, if you're dealing
with performance issues,

00:40:54.555 --> 00:40:57.440
Trace should be one of
the first destinations

00:40:57.440 --> 00:41:00.980
to understand what is happening
within your application

00:41:00.980 --> 00:41:04.350
and to be able to slice and
dice the Trace data so that you

00:41:04.350 --> 00:41:09.400
can actually find the root
cause and find the bottlenecks.

00:41:09.400 --> 00:41:13.510
Error Reporting, as
I've emphasized, pin it.

00:41:13.510 --> 00:41:18.230
If you've got an
application running in GCP,

00:41:18.230 --> 00:41:21.100
go ahead and pin the
Error Reporting page.

00:41:21.100 --> 00:41:23.230
And that's something
that is going

00:41:23.230 --> 00:41:24.390
to be tremendously useful.

00:41:24.390 --> 00:41:30.460
Our customers, whom we
have already released it to

00:41:30.460 --> 00:41:32.130
on a private beta,
have come back

00:41:32.130 --> 00:41:37.940
with phenomenal feedback telling
us how incredibly valuable

00:41:37.940 --> 00:41:39.690
it is.

00:41:39.690 --> 00:41:45.070
And Debug, I think this is one
of those things where-- it's

00:41:45.070 --> 00:41:47.270
one of the best tools that
I've used, personally,

00:41:47.270 --> 00:41:51.360
in terms of stepping through
the behavior of your code

00:41:51.360 --> 00:41:52.870
and understanding
what is happening

00:41:52.870 --> 00:41:56.720
within your application
without really interrupting

00:41:56.720 --> 00:41:59.840
your normal operations.

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:02.760
As I've already talked about
before, all of this stuff

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:06.480
works natively in a
distributed environment.

00:42:06.480 --> 00:42:08.630
We can scale very comfortably.

00:42:08.630 --> 00:42:12.240
We can scale from one
instance, if your application's

00:42:12.240 --> 00:42:14.667
running in one instance to--
many of our customers run

00:42:14.667 --> 00:42:17.000
their application in hundreds
of thousands of instances.

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:20.497
And so we can capture
all this data seamlessly.

00:42:24.100 --> 00:42:27.660
Another aspect of scale to
think about is, say, take Trace,

00:42:27.660 --> 00:42:28.720
for example.

00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:33.150
We have about a billion traces,
approximately a billion traces,

00:42:33.150 --> 00:42:35.010
coming into our
system every day.

00:42:35.010 --> 00:42:37.270
And every one of those
traces is analyzed

00:42:37.270 --> 00:42:40.070
to look for different call
patterns, suboptimal call

00:42:40.070 --> 00:42:40.570
patterns.

00:42:40.570 --> 00:42:43.730
Then we surface that.

00:42:43.730 --> 00:42:45.910
This is tightly integrated
into developer workflows.

00:42:45.910 --> 00:42:47.535
And of course, our
team, as you saw it,

00:42:47.535 --> 00:42:50.750
is actually shipping
features as we talk here.

00:42:50.750 --> 00:42:56.780
So you'll start seeing
links show up between logs

00:42:56.780 --> 00:43:00.560
to traces and Debugger.

00:43:00.560 --> 00:43:03.260
There's already a
bunch of integration

00:43:03.260 --> 00:43:05.490
that has happened
where you can access

00:43:05.490 --> 00:43:09.160
all this data or these tools
directly from your dashboards,

00:43:09.160 --> 00:43:11.470
whether it's your
App Engine dashboard

00:43:11.470 --> 00:43:14.130
or whether it's your
Stackdriver dashboard.

00:43:14.130 --> 00:43:17.450
Incident management
does integrations

00:43:17.450 --> 00:43:20.300
for all these tools within your
incident management systems.

00:43:20.300 --> 00:43:24.490
And I can't highlight enough
about the extensibility here.

00:43:24.490 --> 00:43:27.140
All these tools are publicly
available with our APIs

00:43:27.140 --> 00:43:27.980
as well.

00:43:27.980 --> 00:43:33.990
And so that lets you incorporate
it within your own systems

00:43:33.990 --> 00:43:36.170
so that you can-- let's
say, if you wanted

00:43:36.170 --> 00:43:41.060
to add your own custom spans,
where you wanted to measure

00:43:41.060 --> 00:43:44.300
your own custom workflows, and
you wanted to still leverage

00:43:44.300 --> 00:43:47.760
all the Trace analytics that
we have, then you can do that.

00:43:47.760 --> 00:43:50.540
And of course, the best part
of it is, it's all free.

00:43:50.540 --> 00:43:52.230
We're not charging
anything for this.

00:43:52.230 --> 00:43:54.060
It's available out of the box.

00:43:54.060 --> 00:43:54.530
ERIK MCCLENNEY: But
you know, Sharat,

00:43:54.530 --> 00:43:56.120
it's not just about the cost.

00:43:56.120 --> 00:43:58.370
It's about the value.

00:43:58.370 --> 00:43:59.540
SHARAT SHROFF: You got it.

